1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,160
 Hey everyone, this is Ryan here and welcome back to our oral surgery series.

2
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:12,680
 So far we've talked about indications and contraindications to tooth extraction, why

3
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:17,440
 we would need to perform extractions in the first place, then we talked about the various

4
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:23,120
 instruments utilized for tooth extraction, and now we can finally talk about the exciting

5
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:26,840
 topic of performing a tooth extraction.

6
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:33,400
 The preparatory steps involved and then the actual protocol for a routine tooth extraction.

7
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:38,800
 In the next video we'll talk about surgical extractions and the additional steps that

8
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:45,120
 come with more difficult and complicated procedures.

9
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:50,720
 So there's some preparatory steps we need to take before taking a tooth out, and this

10
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:57,280
 may seem silly, but we need to remove the entire correct tooth.

11
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:03,040
 Because there are serious potential consequences with taking out the wrong tooth and it does

12
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:10,280
 unfortunately happen, so confirm with yourself, with your progress notes, with the patient,

13
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:15,480
 and with your assistant to make sure that you're working on the correct tooth.

14
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:20,000
 Check the tooth condition, you want to see what you're working with.

15
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,360
 Are there deep carries where you'll likely crack the crown off?

16
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:28,800
 Is there an adjacent restoration or crown that we may potentially damage and need to

17
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:30,400
 be careful of?

18
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,640
 Is this a root tip that we will have to remove some bone in order to access?

19
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:38,220
 These are all questions that you should consider beforehand.

20
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:46,160
 Check the radiograph, radiographs are a must, you absolutely need to have a panoramic for

21
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:51,880
 third molars, and a PA for every tooth you're taking out.

22
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:58,720
 It must clearly show the root shape, the root apices, and the surrounding structures.

23
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:05,280
 You want to check the proximity of the inferior alveolar nerve in the mandibular and the proximity

24
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:08,120
 of the sinus for the maxillary teeth.

25
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:14,360
 And this is just because of those complications we talked about in the first video.

26
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:19,560
 We need informed consent, describe alternate treatment options to the patient, including

27
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:25,600
 no treatment, discuss the procedure in layman's terms so they know what to expect, and possible

28
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:31,960
 complications like swelling, bleeding, pain, infection, those sorts of things.

29
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:37,080
 The patient and provider should both be in comfortable positions.

30
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:42,800
 I find that having the patient's mouth at about elbow level is rather comfortable.

31
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:49,060
 A more upright chair position is best for lower teeth and a more reclined position is best

32
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:51,040
 for upper teeth.

33
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:57,560
 We need profound anesthesia, of course this is pretty obvious, but we need to make sure

34
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:59,720
 that the patient's comfortable.

35
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:08,840
 Blocks are preferred where we can use those in addition to local injections and even PDL

36
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:14,520
 injections as well, and palatal injections are needed for the palate if you're working

37
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:22,200
 on a maxillary tooth in order to get that palatal, soft and hard tissue profoundly numb.

38
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:29,840
 And lastly, we should use a throat screen, usually a 4x4 piece of sterile gauze is placed

39
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:35,120
 in the patient's mouth to block anything from falling down into their throat like an extracted

40
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:40,560
 tooth, and many clinicians consider this a mandatory step for all cases.

41
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:46,480
 All right, so those are the basic preparatory steps for an extraction.

42
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:53,240
 Let's talk about the differences briefly between a simple and a surgical extraction.

43
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:59,240
 So as far as coding for procedures, there is a distinct difference between simple and

44
00:03:59,240 --> 00:04:01,240
 surgical extraction.

45
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:07,920
 Simple extractions usually, no incisions are needed, and hence no sutures are needed.

46
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:14,360
 While surgical extractions typically involve a surgical access via elevating a mucoperiosteal

47
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:20,240
 flap, and for flaps, the same rules apply that we talked about in the periodontics videos,

48
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:26,000
 a wide base to maintain vasculature, cut to the line angles of teeth, make the flap sufficiently

49
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,920
 large so you can get good access and visibility, and we'll talk about those in detail in the

50
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:34,040
 next video.

51
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:38,360
 A surgical handpiece to remove bone or section the tooth.

52
00:04:38,360 --> 00:04:43,680
 Now as soon as I pick up a surgical handpiece, it becomes a surgical extraction.

53
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:52,320
 So that's really the limiting step in determining when we get to a surgical extraction point.

54
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:58,600
 A suture is usually needed since we had to open up a flap for surgical visibility and

55
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:00,040
 access.

56
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:04,680
 So again, we'll talk about all these things in much more detail in the next video.

57
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:10,720
 Let's focus on simple, uncomplicated extractions in this video.

58
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:17,320
 So the first step is to sever the soft tissue attachment.

59
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:24,280
 We want to loosen the gingival fibers and the periodontal ligament fibers attached to

60
00:05:24,280 --> 00:05:26,680
 the tooth.

61
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:29,560
 So here we see a periosteal elevator.

62
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:33,920
 We talked about this in the last video on instrumentation.

63
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:40,280
 This would typically be a Woodson or a number nine malt periosteal elevator.

64
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:45,960
 And so when we're doing this first step, it confirms good anesthesia.

65
00:05:45,960 --> 00:05:49,080
 It tells you if the patient is profoundly numb.

66
00:05:49,080 --> 00:05:54,640
 They shouldn't feel anything sharp, but they will feel pressure and pushing.

67
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:59,680
 And make sure that the patient understands this before starting.

68
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:05,440
 By placing the periosteal elevator and pushing and severing that soft tissue attachment

69
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:11,760
 360 degrees around the tooth, it allows for apical placement of the forceps.

70
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:19,020
 By severing those fibers and freeing up access to tooth structure, ideally apical to the

71
00:06:19,020 --> 00:06:21,120
 CEJ.

72
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:26,960
 Certainly apical to the clinical crown that we can see because we want to get as close

73
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:35,800
 to the center of resistance, which is at the center of the embedded root, with the forceps.

74
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:41,600
 So step number two is to luxate that tooth with the dental elevator.

75
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:46,960
 So the last step we use the periosteal elevator, once we sever as much of those fibers as

76
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:51,320
 we can, we can confidently move to this next step.

77
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:58,400
 So the face of the dental elevator blade should be placed against the tooth to be extracted.

78
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:06,640
 We can see this in the top image and this lower left part of this image.

79
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:13,640
 So the face of the blade is against the tooth to be extracted and the back of the blade,

80
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:19,840
 the curved portion, should be against the alveolar crest.

81
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:25,400
 So most of these elevators, like the ones we talked about in the previous video, are

82
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:31,480
 used as a lever with a fulcrum on the alveolar bone.

83
00:07:31,480 --> 00:07:36,920
 Now it's usually inevitable that you will also have some amount of fulcrum on the adjacent

84
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:41,480
 tooth, just because of how the anatomy is, especially if there's a tight contact between

85
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:42,480
 the two.

86
00:07:42,480 --> 00:07:49,400
 But you want to avoid luxating or damaging the crown of this tooth, unless it too is

87
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:51,960
 being extracted.

88
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:57,800
 So the idea with the elevator is to find what's called a purchase point, somewhere where

89
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:05,960
 you can get between bone and the tooth and feel a good amount of resistance as you slowly

90
00:08:05,960 --> 00:08:14,880
 twist the elevator in a clockwise or a counterclockwise direction, so that the blade is gradually

91
00:08:14,880 --> 00:08:22,120
 facing occlusily and the tooth moves slightly up and away from the elevator.

92
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:28,080
 So you can see how in this image we're now moving in a counterclockwise direction to

93
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:31,800
 get that blade to face occlusily.

94
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:36,560
 Now you can move the blade in either direction, here it's showing clockwise, here it's showing

95
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:42,720
 counterclockwise, and you can alternate between those two methods.

96
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:49,240
 But whichever way you stick to, this should be done in a very slow and controlled manner,

97
00:08:49,240 --> 00:08:57,840
 twisting and holding the elevator in a point of resistance for up to 10 seconds.

98
00:08:57,840 --> 00:09:03,880
 Because what we're doing with this step is expanding the alveolus or the socket of that

99
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:11,280
 tooth to allow easy delivery of the entire tooth in the third step.

100
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:18,520
 So it can be good practice to support the alveolar ridge with your opposite hand or support the

101
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:24,000
 tuberosity if you're working on an upper third molar in order to ensure that you're

102
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:29,460
 not applying too much force to the ridge.

103
00:09:29,460 --> 00:09:35,680
 So again we're causing expansion of bone and continued tearing of the peridontal ligament

104
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:38,240
 that's holding that tooth in place.

105
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:43,560
 So I find that being slow and deliberate with these first two steps can make an otherwise

106
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:48,740
 difficult extraction go very very smoothly.

107
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:55,320
 And now our third step to deliver the tooth with forceps and again, here's the key, slow

108
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:56,680
 and deliberate.

109
00:09:56,680 --> 00:10:03,000
 Any forces should be held for several seconds to allow the bone time to expand instead of

110
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:07,120
 just jerking one way and then the next way and then the next way.

111
00:10:07,120 --> 00:10:11,840
 That's how things tend to break and crack and fracture.

112
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:16,120
 So a great concept that I learned in dental school is that the tooth should first be

113
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:19,320
 moved and then removed.

114
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:24,160
 So all of these steps, one, two and three, are working towards the same goal.

115
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:30,160
 We're breaking the peridontal ligament fibers and we're expanding the investing bone sufficiently

116
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:35,880
 to make the tooth nice and mobile and then allow the tooth to be delivered from its socket.

117
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:41,560
 Again while you're doing this, your opposite hand might grab the alveolar process to provide

118
00:10:41,560 --> 00:10:48,140
 tactile information about bone expansion and also support and stabilize the jaw for lower

119
00:10:48,140 --> 00:10:52,040
 teeth if you're not using a bite block.

120
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:57,760
 So when we're grabbing the tooth with our dental forceps, there are several motions

121
00:10:57,760 --> 00:10:59,640
 we can do.

122
00:10:59,640 --> 00:11:04,320
 The outward motion, which is towards the buckle or the labial, is the initial movement for

123
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:06,160
 most permanent teeth.

124
00:11:06,160 --> 00:11:13,120
 An inward or lingual palatal movement is typically the initial movement for most primary teeth

125
00:11:13,120 --> 00:11:18,840
 and that's because of the orientation of the roots of most primary teeth.

126
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:25,960
 A rotary movement can be used as the initial movement for conical rooted teeth like upper

127
00:11:25,960 --> 00:11:30,280
 centrals and lower premolars for example.

128
00:11:30,280 --> 00:11:36,560
 Extended apical pressure should be applied to every tooth with the forceps.

129
00:11:36,560 --> 00:11:41,840
 You want to grab the tooth as apically as possible, like we mentioned before, to get

130
00:11:41,840 --> 00:11:49,080
 as close to the center of resistance as possible and to avoid breaking the crown off.

131
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:54,640
 But we want to avoid excessive pressure in the maxillary molar region and that's one

132
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:59,920
 of those complications we talked about in the first video, displacement into the maxillary

133
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:02,360
 sinus.

134
00:12:02,360 --> 00:12:08,000
 Now there's a bit more technique sensitive method but you can do a figure eight motion

135
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:13,920
 while simultaneously applying apical pressure and that can be very effective as well.

136
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:22,480
 But let's talk about specific teeth and the specific movements you'd make with each one.

137
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:29,520
 So after the periosteal and dental elevators, we want to see the forceps as apically as

138
00:12:29,520 --> 00:12:36,400
 possible around the tooth since we freed up some of those soft tissue attachments that

139
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:39,800
 would otherwise be getting in the way of us accessing this.

140
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:48,680
 So then we would luxate or move the tooth labially in this direction and then lingually

141
00:12:48,680 --> 00:12:51,000
 until it's mobile enough to remove.

142
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:58,080
 So we would go labial with some resistance, hold for a few seconds, then push it.

143
00:12:58,080 --> 00:13:03,160
 And then lingually hold for a few seconds and it's slow and deliberate.

144
00:13:03,160 --> 00:13:09,080
 Nothing is incredibly forceful but you want to always apply some apical pressure in order

145
00:13:09,080 --> 00:13:14,320
 to get the tips of these forceps as apically seated as possible.

146
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:20,880
 Also rotary motion can be used because of these conical roots and that can help to break

147
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:25,600
 a lot of the more apically located PDL fibers.

148
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:30,640
 And then the tooth can be removed once it's sufficiently mobile.

149
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:36,240
 Upper canines, similar concept to the last slide, we want to move the tooth labially

150
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:41,520
 then lingually then some rotation with apical pressure.

151
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:48,560
 But canines have very long roots and the canine eminence is often adherent to the root, making

152
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:51,680
 it more difficult to extract.

153
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:58,080
 So it may require a mucoperiosteal flap and you make a trough around the tooth to avoid

154
00:13:58,080 --> 00:14:01,280
 removing the entire bony socket.

155
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:05,920
 And these are all things we're going to talk about in the next video.

156
00:14:05,920 --> 00:14:08,800
 How about upper first premolars?

157
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:14,400
 So we can move the tooth buckily, hold for a few seconds, then lingual, then buckle and

158
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:15,680
 so on.

159
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:21,440
 The difference here is that upper first premolars often have these two roots that don't

160
00:14:21,440 --> 00:14:24,580
 split until the apical third of the tooth.

161
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:31,480
 So these are very easy to crack off, especially if you're applying rotational force.

162
00:14:31,480 --> 00:14:37,360
 So you might notice we don't have that rotational arrow in this image and that's because as

163
00:14:37,360 --> 00:14:45,960
 a general rule, we do not rotate any teeth that have more than one root.

164
00:14:45,960 --> 00:14:50,160
 And this is something that we would confirm with that periapical radiograph that we took

165
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:52,320
 prior to the extraction.

166
00:14:52,320 --> 00:14:56,680
 So you can go in knowing that I'm not going to use rotational force, we're just going

167
00:14:56,680 --> 00:15:02,320
 to use buckle and lingual force.

168
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:04,400
 How about upper second premolars?

169
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:11,080
 Now these don't typically have that second root they can, so again just be cognizant

170
00:15:11,080 --> 00:15:20,600
 of that and use those force movements that we've talked about.

171
00:15:20,600 --> 00:15:22,560
 How about upper molars?

172
00:15:22,560 --> 00:15:26,880
 So now we're talking about teeth with multiple roots and so we have to be a little bit more

173
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:33,640
 careful and these are just more difficult by virtue of having more peridontal ligament

174
00:15:33,640 --> 00:15:39,320
 surface area or volume that is holding the tooth in place.

175
00:15:39,320 --> 00:15:47,080
 So for the first molar we want to start with a buckle movement and notice that it can be

176
00:15:47,080 --> 00:15:48,360
 a little bit confusing.

177
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:55,360
 The previous images buckle was shown as being to the left but it's to the right in these

178
00:15:55,360 --> 00:15:56,600
 photos.

179
00:15:56,600 --> 00:16:02,640
 So we start with buckle movement and then a lingual or palatal movement and we want to

180
00:16:02,640 --> 00:16:07,760
 favor buckle pressure in the upper molars.

181
00:16:07,760 --> 00:16:13,920
 And that's because the palatal root, which is shown here, may be forced into the sinus

182
00:16:13,920 --> 00:16:20,520
 if we're not careful and apply excessive palatal pressure and excessive apical pressure.

183
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:26,600
 We can end up forcing that long bulky palatal root into the maxillary sinus.

184
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:33,920
 So for upper molars we tend to focus on buckle pressure and delivering the tooth in an outward

185
00:16:33,920 --> 00:16:35,400
 buckle direction.

186
00:16:35,400 --> 00:16:39,640
 Alright, so let's talk about the lower teeth.

187
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:44,440
 Lower incisors and canines follow a similar concept.

188
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:51,040
 The bone is thin on both sides of these teeth so be careful of breaks.

189
00:16:51,040 --> 00:16:56,880
 Roots are a little bit less conical and circular than the upper anterior teeth so use less

190
00:16:56,880 --> 00:17:00,920
 rotation and more buckle movement.

191
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:07,640
 And this is also the most common site of crowding which can make access with the forceps more

192
00:17:07,640 --> 00:17:11,120
 difficult.

193
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:16,520
 Lower premolars, these are probably among the easiest teeth to extract.

194
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:22,520
 Rotation is most important due to these very conical and they tend to be straight single

195
00:17:22,520 --> 00:17:23,520
 roots.

196
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:29,120
 So lower premolars can be very, very nice to extract and again we see that rotational

197
00:17:29,120 --> 00:17:32,360
 arrow here.

198
00:17:32,360 --> 00:17:35,960
 Now lower molars are exact opposite.

199
00:17:35,960 --> 00:17:39,440
 These are among the hardest teeth to extract.

200
00:17:39,440 --> 00:17:48,280
 Since the roots are often divergent and the bone is densest and the posterior mandible.

201
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:55,560
 So here we see a mesial view of or distal view of a lower molar and we're seeing this

202
00:17:55,560 --> 00:18:00,800
 buckle pressure being applied first and then the lingual pressure and of course no rotational

203
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:03,640
 pressure because we have multiple roots.

204
00:18:03,640 --> 00:18:09,960
 So these you have to be very slow and steady again focusing on apical pressure, apical

205
00:18:09,960 --> 00:18:15,080
 seating of the forceps and using something like a cow horn forceps in order to engage

206
00:18:15,080 --> 00:18:21,400
 the bifurcation area.

207
00:18:21,400 --> 00:18:26,840
 So after the tooth is removed, what can we do after the tooth is removed to promote the

208
00:18:26,840 --> 00:18:30,200
 best healing of the tooth socket?

209
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:35,240
 So remember we expanded the alveolus as part of our extraction.

210
00:18:35,240 --> 00:18:41,400
 That was one of our main features and treatment philosophies was expanding that bone.

211
00:18:41,400 --> 00:18:47,400
 So it's good practice to squeeze the buckle and lingual cortical plates together with

212
00:18:47,400 --> 00:18:50,640
 your fingers to facilitate better healing.

213
00:18:50,640 --> 00:18:56,560
 So you'd want to bend back the bone unless orthonautics or an implant is planned in that

214
00:18:56,560 --> 00:18:58,680
 area in the future.

215
00:18:58,680 --> 00:19:00,200
 Bone is flexible to an extent.

216
00:19:00,200 --> 00:19:06,000
 We don't have to force it especially if the cortical plates are really thin but we can

217
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:13,400
 bend that bone back just slightly with our fingers in order to make that alveolus a little

218
00:19:13,400 --> 00:19:15,320
 bit smaller.

219
00:19:15,320 --> 00:19:20,240
 But we want to avoid doing this if ortho or implant is planned in the future because smashing

220
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:26,420
 the cortical plates together will inevitably cause undesirable cortical anchorage and

221
00:19:26,420 --> 00:19:32,760
 thinning of the buckle lingual dimension of alveolar bone which is not desirable for implant

222
00:19:32,760 --> 00:19:38,880
 placement or for future orthodontics if we're going to be moving teeth through that area.

223
00:19:38,880 --> 00:19:45,760
 Now after an extraction is done we have to clean up the scene and do some CSI or crime

224
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:47,120
 scene investigation.

225
00:19:47,120 --> 00:19:54,760
 So I remember CSI as our acronym here stands for curitage, smooth bone and irrigate.

226
00:19:54,760 --> 00:20:02,640
 So when curating we want to remove residual cysts, radicular cysts, granulation tissue,

227
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:07,160
 root and bone debris, whatever might be in that socket and clean it out.

228
00:20:07,160 --> 00:20:12,260
 And we do have to be careful about anatomy in the area like if we have the sinus close

229
00:20:12,260 --> 00:20:18,480
 by or the inferior alveolar nerve running close by as well.

230
00:20:18,480 --> 00:20:25,000
 So we get some nice bleeding into the socket and everything is cleaned out.

231
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:30,600
 We can also smooth the bone with a bone file or ringer pliers.

232
00:20:30,600 --> 00:20:36,520
 So we're talking about bone removers and this is so we can avoid having really sharp areas

233
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:42,400
 on the ridge that can irritate the patient and also make healing a little bit less than

234
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:44,080
 pleasant.

235
00:20:44,080 --> 00:20:49,680
 And lastly we irrigate the socket to flush everything out, again remove any of that bone

236
00:20:49,680 --> 00:20:58,720
 debris or root debris that was left with some sterile saline and typically a monoget syringe.

